# ThreadSanitizer suppressions — AetherSDR CI
# ============================================================================
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ============================================================================
# The weekly Sanitizers workflow failed EVERY RUN from 2026-07-20 onward (and
# on 2026-07-06), reporting a ThreadSanitizer data race. Seven consecutive
# failure emails were sent. The race is NOT in AetherSDR.
#
# Every frame of both reported races is inside PulseAudio's own library:
#
#   WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
#     Atomic read  ... pa_mutex_lock  (libpulsecommon-16.1.so)
#     Previous write ... pa_xmalloc   (libpulsecommon-16.1.so)
#     Thread T1 'threaded-ml' created by pa_thread_new (libpulsecommon-16.1.so)
#   SUMMARY: ThreadSanitizer: data race (libpulsecommon-16.1.so) in pa_mutex_lock
#
# Not one AetherSDR translation unit appears in any stack. It is triggered by
# the CI container having no audio device and no writable HOME, so
# pa_context_connect() fails and PulseAudio races against itself during
# teardown:
#
#   Home directory not accessible: Permission denied
#   PulseAudioService: pa_context_connect() failed
#   [SKIP] default output / RX   (no device on this runner)
#
# 🔴 The tests' OWN assertions passed ("2/2 checks passed"). They were recorded
# as failures purely because TSan's warning made the process exit non-zero.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHY SUPPRESS RATHER THAN FIX
# ============================================================================
# The bug is in a third-party shared library we do not build and cannot patch
# from this repository. Suppression is the mechanism TSan provides for exactly
# this case. It is NOT a way of quietening our own races.
#
# ⚠️ SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW — the library is named explicitly. A blanket
# rule (e.g. `race:*`) would hide real AetherSDR races and turn this whole
# workflow into theatre. If a race in our own code ever appears, this file will
# NOT hide it. Do not widen these patterns.
#
# 📌 If the CI image ever gains a working audio device, or PulseAudio is
# replaced by PipeWire in the container, re-test with this file removed before
# assuming it is still needed.
# ============================================================================

# The racing library itself.
race:libpulsecommon
# Races reached THROUGH PulseAudio entry points (its internal 'threaded-ml'
# mainloop thread), which is how the reported stacks are rooted.
called_from_lib:libpulsecommon
called_from_lib:libpulse.so
